window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"title": "About Us", "url": "about.html", "desc": "About i.zino concept – our story, approach and commitment to building lasting commercial relationships in Nigeria.", "text": "About Us Building strong, durable working relationships between parties who share mutual commercial interests. Who We Are i.zino concept is a Nigerian service and consultancy firm headquartered in Warri, Delta State. We focus on practical commercial outcomes: signed retainership agreements, improved customer-service systems, and clear documentation that helps businesses operate confidently. Our work centres on introducing major clients—especially in the oil and gas sector—to hospitals and clinics for medical retainership arrangements, and on providing consultancy and customer-service support to organisations of every size. What Guides Us We believe lasting business relationships are built on clarity, reliability and genuine value for both sides. Whether we are facilitating a retainership contract or helping a company redesign how it handles enquiries and complaints, our aim is the same: results that clients can measure and trust. Client Loyalty We design services that turn first-time interactions into long-term relationships. Local Knowledge Deep understanding of the Nigerian business environment, especially oil & gas and healthcare. Practical Delivery We prioritise completed agreements, clear documentation and usable systems over theory. Our Approach Every engagement begins with listening. We take time to understand the operational needs of hospitals, the compliance requirements of energy companies, and the customer-experience goals of private businesses. From there we propose concrete next steps—introductions, draft agreements, process recommendations or documentation support—and stay involved until the work is complete. Start a Conversation Business details i.zino concept Warri, Delta State, Nigeria Email: i.zinoconcept@gmail.com About our Resources section The articles in our Resources section are written by the i.zino concept team from our day-to-day work on retainership introductions, registration support and customer-service projects. They are general information, not legal, medical or tax advice. Each article shows the date it was published or last reviewed, and we update articles when regulations or practice change. If you spot an error or an out-of-date detail, please email us and we will correct it."}, {"title": "Resources: Retainership, Registration & Customer Service Guides", "url": "blog.html", "desc": "Practical guides from i.zino concept on medical retainership agreements, choosing hospitals, occupational health, petroleum-sector registration in Nigeria and customer complaint handling.", "text": "Resources Practical guides on retainership, workplace health, registration and customer service in Nigeria. Browse by topic Medical Retainership (3) Workplace Health & Safety (2) Registration & Compliance (1) Customer Service (1) Medical Retainership Agreements, HMO comparison and choosing a hospital. 8 November 2026 · 7 min read What a Medical Retainership Agreement Should Cover: A Clause-by-Clause Guide A medical retainership agreement is only as useful as its detail. Here are the clauses that matter, how pricing models differ, and what both the company and the hospital should insist on before signing. Read the full guide → 8 November 2026 · 6 min read Medical Retainership or HMO? How Nigerian Companies Can Decide Two common ways to provide staff healthcare, and no universal winner. We compare cost predictability, staff choice, administrative load and control over service levels. Read the full guide → 8 November 2026 · 7 min read How to Choose a Hospital for a Corporate Retainership: A Due-Diligence Checklist Before a company signs a retainership, it should visit, test and score the hospital. Here is a checklist covering licensing, emergencies, diagnostics, specialists, power, billing and references. Read the full guide → Workplace Health & Safety Medical examinations and emergency planning for employers. 8 November 2026 · 6 min read Pre-Employment and Periodic Medical Examinations: A Practical Guide for Employers Medical examinations protect both the worker and the company, but only if the purpose, scope, outcomes and confidentiality rules are defined in advance. Here is how to set them up. Read the full guide → 8 November 2026 · 6 min read Building a Medical Emergency Plan for Remote and Field Staff When the nearest hospital is hours away, the plan matters more than the contract. A step-by-step approach to risk assessment, communication, transport, first aid and drills. Read the full guide → Registration & Compliance Getting a service company ready for oil and gas work. 8 November 2026 · 8 min read Petroleum-Sector Registration in Nigeria: A Practical Document Checklist for Service Companies Registration for oil and gas work is a chain of dependencies. This checklist shows the order that works, what has changed since the DPR era, and the mistakes that cause repeat submissions. Read the full guide → Customer Service Handling enquiries and complaints well. 8 November 2026 · 6 min read A Simple Complaint-Handling Process Any Small Business Can Run Most lost customers were not lost to a competitor. They were lost to silence. This six-step process, with sample response-time targets and a message template, can be run by a small team. Read the full guide → Looking for something specific? FAQ · Glossary Need tailored advice? These articles are general. If you have a specific retainership, registration or customer-service challenge, we can discuss it directly. Contact Us"}, {"title": "How to Choose a Hospital for a Corporate Retainership: A Due-Diligence Checklist", "url": "choosing-a-hospital-for-corporate-retainership.html", "desc": "A practical due-diligence checklist for Nigerian companies choosing a private hospital or clinic for a corporate medical retainership: licensing, emergency capacity, diagnostics, specialists, billing and a scoring method.", "text": "Resources › Checklist · Hospital Selection How to Choose a Hospital for a Corporate Retainership: A Due-Diligence Checklist By the i.zino concept team · Published 8 November 2026 · 7 min read Choosing the hospital is the most consequential decision in a medical retainership. The best-drafted contract cannot make up for a facility that cannot cope with your staff's needs at 2 a.m. This checklist is the kind of due diligence we encourage companies to carry out before we facilitate an agreement, and it works just as well if you are doing the search yourself. Step 1: Define what you need before you look Write down the realistic demands: the number of employees and dependants, where they live and work, the most likely conditions (general outpatient care, maternity, injuries, occupational health), and any special requirements such as pre-employment or periodic medicals. Without this, you will compare hospitals on impressions rather than fit. Step 2: Check the basics Registration and licensing. Ask for evidence that the facility is registered and licensed to operate by the relevant state health authority, and that its doctors and nurses hold current registration with their professional councils. Ask to see, not just be told. Ownership and standing. Confirm the registered name of the business, who runs it, and how long it has operated. Insurance. Ask whether it carries professional indemnity cover. Step 3: Inspect the facility in person A visit tells you more than a brochure. Look at the following, and take notes: Emergency capacity. Is there genuine round-the-clock cover with a doctor on site or on rapid call? How many beds are available for observation and admission? Is there an ambulance, and who staffs it? Diagnostics. Are laboratory and basic imaging done in-house, and how quickly are results returned? Outsourced tests add delay. Specialists. Which specialties are available full-time, which are visiting, and on what days? For example, obstetrics and gynaecology, paediatrics, general surgery, orthopaedics, and ophthalmology. Pharmacy. Is it stocked for common prescriptions, so that patients are not sent out to buy drugs? Power and water. What is the backup if the grid fails? A hospital without reliable backup power cannot guarantee service. Hygiene and infection control. General cleanliness, handling of waste, and separation of patient areas are visible within minutes. Patient flow. How are registration, waiting and triage handled? Would a corporate patient be seen promptly? Records. Are patient records organised and kept confidentially? Step 4: Test the corporate side of the business Many hospitals are strong clinically but have never managed a corporate client. Ask: Is there a named corporate-services or account officer? Can they produce itemised invoices that match authorisations? How do they handle pre-authorisation, and referrals to tertiary facilities? Can they provide periodic utilisation reports? What is their position on payment terms? Step 5: Take references Ask for the names of other corporate clients and contact two or three. Ask them about response times, billing accuracy and how problems were resolved. Be sceptical of a hospital that cannot or will not provide any. Step 6: Score and compare Put the findings into a simple matrix so the decision is transparent and can be explained to management. Weight the criteria according to your needs, for instance emergency capacity higher for a field workforce, maternity and paediatrics higher for a family-heavy staff. Criterion Weight (1–5) Hospital A (0–5) Hospital B (0–5) Licensing and standing 5 Emergency and admission capacity 5 Diagnostics (lab and imaging) 4 Specialist coverage 4 Backup power and facilities 3 Corporate billing and reporting 4 Location and access for staff 3 References 3 Multiply each score by its weight and add the totals. The highest total is a starting point for discussion, not an automatic winner. Step 7: Start with a pilot or a review period Where possible, agree a shorter initial term or a formal review after the first three to six months. Real usage will show whether waiting times, billing and communication match what was promised, and the contract should allow both sides to adjust. A note for hospitals If you run a hospital or clinic and want corporate retainerships, the same checklist is your preparation. Have your licences ready, designate a corporate account officer, prepare a clear tariff and a sample utilisation report, and be honest about the capacity you can commit. A smaller commitment kept reliably wins more renewals than a large one that is missed. This is the kind of preparation that makes our introductions between companies and hospitals go smoothly, and you are welcome to get in touch if you want to discuss it. This checklist is general guidance and not a substitute for professional medical, legal or regulatory advice. About this article. Prepared by the i.zino concept team in Warri, Delta State, Nigeria, from our work on medical and business retainership introductions, registration support and customer-service projects. Published 8 November 2026. Spotted an error or an out-of-date detail? Email i.zinoconcept@gmail.com and we will correct it. See our editorial note . Need tailored advice? If you have a specific retainership, registration or customer-service challenge, we can discuss it directly. Contact Us More guides What a Medical Retainership Agreement Should Cover Medical Retainership or HMO? Petroleum-Sector Registration: A Document Checklist A Simple Complaint-Handling Process for Small Businesses Pre-Employment and Periodic Medical Examinations A Medical Emergency Plan for Remote and Field Staff"}, {"title": "Contact", "url": "contact.html", "desc": "Contact i.zino concept in Warri, Nigeria for retainership introductions, consultancy and customer service support.", "text": "Contact Us Tell us about your organisation and how we can help. Send a Message Use the form below or reach us through the channels listed. We aim to respond within one to two business days. Full Name * Email Address * Organisation Subject Medical / Business Retainership Customer Service Support Consultancy Business Registration / Documentation General Enquiry Message * Send Message By submitting you agree that we may use the details you provide to respond to your enquiry. See our Privacy Policy . i.zino concept Location Warri, Delta State, Nigeria Email i.zinoconcept@gmail.com Online Facebook YouTube Client reviews Read or leave a review What to expect We review every genuine enquiry. Please include your organisation name and a short description of the outcome you are looking for so we can reply with relevant next steps."}, {"title": "Cookie Policy", "url": "cookies.html", "desc": "How izinoconcept.github.io uses cookies for analytics and Google AdSense advertising, and how you can manage or opt out of them.", "text": "Cookie Policy Last updated 8 November 2026 This policy explains how izinoconcept.github.io uses cookies and similar technologies. It should be read with our Privacy Policy . 1. What are cookies? Cookies are small text files stored on your device when you visit a website. They help a site work, remember preferences, measure usage and, where ads are shown, deliver and measure advertising. 2. Cookies and similar technologies on this site Purpose Provider What it does Analytics Google Analytics / Google Tag Manager Counts visits and shows which pages are used, so we can improve the site. Data is aggregated; it does not tell us who you are. Advertising Google AdSense (and Google's advertising partners) Delivers and measures ads, limits how often you see the same ad and, unless you opt out, may personalise ads based on your browsing. Fonts Google Fonts Loads the typefaces used on the site. Your IP address is sent to Google when the fonts load. Contact form FormSubmit Delivers messages you submit to our inbox. We do not control third-party cookies. Their use is governed by the providers' own policies. 3. Managing your choices Your browser. Most browsers let you block or delete cookies in their settings. Blocking some cookies may affect how pages work. Personalised ads. Opt out through Google Ads Settings or aboutads.info . Analytics. Google provides a browser add-on that stops its analytics from collecting data about your visits. Consent. Where the law requires consent for non-essential cookies, we ask for it and you can change your choice later. 4. Updates We may update this policy from time to time. The date above shows the latest version. 5. Contact Questions about cookies: i.zinoconcept@gmail.com ."}, {"title": "A Simple Complaint-Handling Process Any Small Business Can Run", "url": "customer-complaint-handling-process-small-business.html", "desc": "A practical, six-step complaint-handling process for small Nigerian businesses: acknowledge, log, assign, resolve, follow up and learn, with response-time targets, a message template and metrics to track.", "text": "Resources › Customer Service · Practical Guide A Simple Complaint-Handling Process Any Small Business Can Run By the i.zino concept team · Published 8 November 2026 · 6 min read Most businesses do not lose customers because of one mistake. They lose them because of what happens next: a message nobody answers, a promise nobody follows up, a complaint passed between three people. The good news is that complaint handling is a process, and a small team can run a good one without special software. Customer-service operations are one of the three core things we help organisations build, so here is the framework we recommend. Why it matters commercially A customer who complains is still talking to you. A well-handled complaint often builds more trust than a transaction that went smoothly, because the customer sees how you behave under pressure. A badly handled one gets told to friends and colleagues, and in small markets and small industries, word travels quickly. The six-step process 1. Acknowledge quickly The first response matters most, and it does not need to contain the solution. It needs to confirm that a real person has received the complaint and tell the customer what will happen next and by when. Silence is the most damaging response. 2. Log every complaint in one place A shared spreadsheet is enough to start. For each complaint, record the date and channel (phone, WhatsApp, email, in person), the customer's name and contact, a clear description of the problem, who owns it, and the status. If complaints live in different people's phones, you will never see patterns, and customers will be asked to repeat themselves. 3. Assign one owner and a severity level Every complaint needs a single named owner who is responsible until it is closed, even if others help. Also classify severity so urgent issues are not stuck behind minor ones: High: safety, health, legal or large financial impact, or a major client at risk. Medium: a service failure with a clear remedy. Low: a minor inconvenience or a general suggestion. 4. Resolve within a stated time Set internal targets and tell customers realistic dates. The numbers below are examples to adapt to your own capacity, not standards: Severity First response Target resolution High Within 1 hour in business hours Within 1 working day, or a clear interim fix Medium Within 1 working day Within 3 to 5 working days Low Within 2 working days Within 10 working days If you cannot meet a date, tell the customer before it passes, and give a new one. 5. Follow up after resolution Do not close the complaint when you think it is fixed. Close it when the customer confirms. A short message a few days later, asking whether everything is working as expected, costs nothing and catches the problems that would otherwise resurface as a lost account. 6. Learn from the pattern Once a month, review the log. Which problems repeat? Which staff, products, processes or locations appear most often? Fix the cause, not just the incident. This is where complaint handling stops being a cost and starts to improve the business. A message template for the first response Dear [Name], thank you for telling us about [short description]. I am sorry for the trouble this has caused. I have taken ownership of this and will [next action] by [day and time]. If you need me before then, you can reach me on [contact]. Regards, [Your name], [Company]. It does four things in a few lines: it thanks, it apologises sincerely without defensiveness, it names the owner, and it commits to a time. Five habits that separate good teams from average ones Agree what \"good\" looks like. A one-page checklist that every staff member can follow removes guesswork. Tone, response times and escalation rules should be written down. Give staff authority. If the frontline can only say \"I will ask my manager\", resolution will always be slow. Decide in advance what a staff member can fix on the spot, such as a small refund, a replacement or a rebooked appointment. Keep one voice across channels. A customer who complains on WhatsApp and again by phone should meet the same owner and the same story. Separate the person from the problem. Customers are often upset about the issue, not about you. Do not take it personally, and do not argue. Thank the complainers. The ones who tell you the problem are doing you a favour. Most unhappy customers simply leave. Metrics worth tracking Average time to first response Average time to resolution, by severity Number of complaints reopened (a sign of fixes that did not hold) Repeat complaints by type Customer-confirmed satisfaction after closure Four or five numbers reviewed monthly are better than twenty that nobody reads. Where to start this week You do not need to build everything at once. Create the shared log, name an owner for each channel, adopt the first-response template, and review the log at the end of the month. If you want help designing a framework for your organisation, covering enquiry handling, complaint resolution and communication standards, see our customer-service services or get in touch . About this article. Prepared by the i.zino concept team in Warri, Delta State, Nigeria, from our work on medical and business retainership introductions, registration support and customer-service projects. Published 8 November 2026. Spotted an error or an out-of-date detail? Email i.zinoconcept@gmail.com and we will correct it. See our editorial note . Need tailored advice? If you have a specific retainership, registration or customer-service challenge, we can discuss it directly. Contact Us More guides What a Medical Retainership Agreement Should Cover Medical Retainership or HMO? Choosing a Hospital for a Corporate Retainership Petroleum-Sector Registration: A Document Checklist Pre-Employment and Periodic Medical Examinations A Medical Emergency Plan for Remote and Field Staff"}, {"title": "Frequently Asked Questions", "url": "faq.html", "desc": "Answers to common questions about i.zino concept, medical retainership agreements, HMO cover, petroleum-sector registration and how we work.", "text": "Frequently Asked Questions Straight answers about retainership, registration and how we work. What does i.zino concept do? We help organisations in three areas: customer-service operations, medical and business retainership introductions (connecting companies, particularly in oil and gas, with hospitals and clinics), and consultancy, including documentation support for industry registration. Are you a hospital, an HMO or a law firm? No. We facilitate introductions, agreement drafting support and documentation. Medical care is provided by the hospitals themselves, and formal legal advice should come from a lawyer qualified in Nigeria. What is a medical retainership? It is an agreement under which a company designates a hospital or clinic to look after its staff's healthcare needs on agreed terms. Read our guide to what a retainership agreement should cover . How is a retainership different from HMO cover? An HMO manages a network of hospitals and takes part of the financial risk in return for a premium. A retainership is a direct arrangement with a chosen hospital. We compare them in Medical Retainership or HMO? How do I choose a hospital for a retainership? Check licensing, emergency capacity, diagnostics, specialists, backup power and corporate billing, and visit in person. Our due-diligence checklist walks through each step. Which regulator handles oil and gas service permits now? The Department of Petroleum Resources (DPR) was scrapped in 2021. Its functions passed to the NUPRC (upstream) and the NMDPRA (midstream and downstream). Which one applies depends on your service category. See our registration checklist . Do you guarantee that an application will be approved? No. Approval decisions belong to the relevant agencies. We help you prepare accurate, complete documentation in the right order so that avoidable delays and repeat submissions are reduced. How long does NSITF registration take? NSITF indicates that a compliance certificate is typically processed within about five working days once all requirements are submitted and payment is verified. Your own timeline depends on how complete your documents are. Where are you based and how do I contact you? We are based in Warri, Delta State, Nigeria. Email i.zinoconcept@gmail.com or use the form on our contact page . What happens to the details I send you? We use them to respond to your enquiry. See our Privacy Policy for how personal data is handled. Your question not answered? Send it to us or try the site search . Related Glossary of terms All guides Our services"}, {"title": "Glossary: Retainership, Registration and Oil & Gas Terms", "url": "glossary.html", "desc": "Plain-English definitions of terms used in Nigerian medical retainership, HMO cover, employer compliance and petroleum-sector registration, from capitation to NUPRC.", "text": "Glossary Plain-English definitions of terms used in our guides. CAC The Corporate Affairs Commission, the registrar of companies in Nigeria. Capitation A payment model in which a fixed amount is paid per covered person, regardless of how often that person uses the service. DPR (defunct) The former Department of Petroleum Resources, scrapped in 2021. Its regulatory functions passed to NUPRC and NMDPRA. ECA 2010 / Employees' Compensation Scheme The Employees' Compensation Act 2010 sets up a scheme, run by the NSITF, that compensates employees for work-related injury, disease or death. Employers contribute 1% of total monthly payroll. Fee-for-service A payment model in which each service is billed separately at an agreed price. Fitness for work A doctor's conclusion on whether a person can safely do a particular job. See our guide to medical examinations . HMO Health Maintenance Organisation: an organisation that arranges and pays for healthcare through a network of providers in return for a premium. HSE Health, Safety and Environment, the policies and practices that protect people and the environment at work. ITF The Industrial Training Fund, which collects training contributions from liable employers. NCDMB The Nigerian Content Development and Monitoring Board, which oversees local-content requirements in the oil and gas industry. NMDPRA The Nigerian Midstream and Downstream Petroleum Regulatory Authority, regulator for midstream and downstream petroleum operations. NRS The Nigeria Revenue Service, which replaced the Federal Inland Revenue Service (FIRS) on 1 January 2026. NSITF The Nigeria Social Insurance Trust Fund, which administers the Employees' Compensation Scheme. NUPRC The Nigerian Upstream Petroleum Regulatory Commission, regulator for upstream petroleum operations. Occupational health The branch of healthcare concerned with workers' health in relation to their work, including examinations and exposure monitoring. PenCom The National Pension Commission, regulator of the pension system. Compliance evidence is often requested in tender pre-qualification. Pre-authorisation Approval, given before treatment, that a service will be covered and paid for. Referral Sending a patient from one provider to another, such as a specialist or a tertiary hospital. Retainership An arrangement in which a provider is engaged on agreed terms to serve a client over a period. See our guide . Service-level agreement (SLA) A written commitment on how quickly and how well a service will be delivered, such as response times. TIN Tax Identification Number. Utilisation report A periodic summary of how much a client's staff used a provider's services, usually reported in aggregate. Definitions are simplified for general understanding. Check the relevant agency or a qualified adviser for the current legal position. Related FAQ All guides Search the site"}, {"title": "i.zino concept | Customer Service, Retainership & Consultancy", "url": "index.html", "desc": "i.zino concept provides customer service operations, medical and business retainership introductions, consultancy, and general contracts for oil & gas, healthcare and businesses in Nigeria.", "text": "Professional Customer Service & Business Consultancy i.zino concept connects oil & gas companies, hospitals and businesses through retainership agreements, consultancy and quality customer-service operations across Nigeria. Get in Touch Our Services What We Do Practical services that help organisations win and keep clients Medical & Business Retainership We introduce oil & gas and corporate clients to hospitals and clinics and facilitate medical/business retainership agreements that benefit both parties. Customer Service Operations We design and deliver customer-service systems that turn one-time buyers into loyal clients and protect brand reputation. Consultancy & Contracts Business registration, documentation support, proposal development and general contracts for companies of all sizes. View All Services Who We Serve Focused support for organisations that need reliable introductions and service delivery Oil & Gas Companies Operators and service companies seeking medical retainership partners and reliable local support. Hospitals & Clinics Private hospitals and specialist clinics looking for corporate retainership contracts. SMEs & Corporates Businesses that need professional customer-service frameworks and documentation support. Healthcare Providers Medical centres seeking structured agreements with employers and energy-sector clients. About i.zino concept Based in Warri, Nigeria, i.zino concept specialises in building durable working relationships between parties who share mutual commercial interests. Our core work involves introducing major clients in the oil and gas sector and corporate organisations to hospitals for medical retainership arrangements, and delivering practical consultancy and customer-service solutions that keep clients coming back. We combine local knowledge with a clear focus on results: signed agreements, smoother service delivery and measurable client loyalty. Learn More About Us From Our Resources Practical guides on retainership, registration and customer service Guide · Medical Retainership What a Medical Retainership Agreement Should Cover A medical retainership agreement is only as useful as its detail. Here are the clauses that matter, how pricing models differ, and what both the company and the hospital should insist on before signing. Read the guide → Guide · Employee Healthcare Medical Retainership or HMO? Two common ways to provide staff healthcare, and no universal winner. We compare cost predictability, staff choice, administrative load and control over service levels. Read the guide → Checklist · Hospital Selection Choosing a Hospital for a Corporate Retainership Before a company signs a retainership, it should visit, test and score the hospital. Here is a checklist covering licensing, emergencies, diagnostics, specialists, power, billing and references. Read the guide → All Resources Ready to discuss a retainership or consultancy project? Tell us about your organisation and the outcome you need. We respond promptly. Contact Us Today"}, {"title": "Building a Medical Emergency Plan for Remote and Field Staff", "url": "medical-emergency-plan-for-remote-and-field-staff.html", "desc": "How Nigerian employers can build a practical medical emergency plan for staff at remote, field or offshore-support sites: risk assessment, contacts, transport, first aid, hospital arrangements and drills.", "text": "Resources › Guide · Workplace Health & Safety Building a Medical Emergency Plan for Remote and Field Staff By the i.zino concept team · Published 8 November 2026 · 6 min read For a team in an office in a city, a medical emergency usually means calling an ambulance or driving to a nearby hospital. For staff at a remote site, on the road, or at a facility reached by water, the same emergency can mean long delays and hard decisions. A written medical emergency plan, tested and understood by everyone, is what turns a retainership with a hospital from a document into a working safety net. Step 1: Assess the risks at each location List where your people work and what could realistically go wrong there: road accidents, injuries from equipment or heavy lifting, heat illness, snake or insect bites, sudden illness such as a heart problem, and, for some sites, fire or chemical exposure. Note how far each site is from the nearest capable facility and how long the journey takes in good and bad conditions, including the rainy season and water or road access. The plan should be specific to each location, not a generic document. Step 2: Identify the right facilities For each site, name three levels of care: the nearest place that can stabilise a patient, the hospital that can provide definitive treatment, and an alternative if the first is unavailable. Check what each can actually do and at what hours. Your retainership hospital may not be the nearest, so the plan must show how it fits: for example, as the destination for non-urgent care and follow-up, while the nearest stabilising facility handles the first hour. Step 3: Prepare first response on site Trained first aiders. Enough on every shift, with current training. Stocked first-aid kits and basic emergency equipment , checked on a schedule, with items that match the risks you identified. Clear roles. Who calls for help, who stays with the patient, who meets the transport and who informs the family and management. Step 4: Plan communication An emergency plan fails if nobody can call for help. Check mobile coverage on site and have a fallback such as a radio or satellite option where coverage is poor. Put a short emergency contact sheet in every vehicle and work area: site supervisor, company emergency lead, hospital emergency line and ambulance or transport provider. Keep an up-to-date list of next-of-kin contacts and known allergies or conditions, stored with appropriate confidentiality. Step 5: Arrange transport Decide in advance how a patient gets from the site to care. Options might include a dedicated vehicle, a contracted ambulance service, or boat transfer, depending on the terrain. Confirm who pays and who is authorised to call it out, because delays caused by uncertainty over authority cost time. If you rely on a third party, test their response time rather than accepting a promise. Step 6: Connect the plan to your hospital agreement Make sure the hospital knows the plan exists. Share the site list, expected arrival routes and a point of contact, and agree what the hospital will do on receiving a call: preparing a team, giving advice by phone, and accepting patients without waiting for payment authorisation in a genuine emergency. These expectations belong in the written agreement; see our guide to retainership agreement clauses for emergency and referral wording. Step 7: Train and rehearse Run a drill at least once a year, and after any change of site, contractor or staffing. A simple scenario, such as a worker with a serious injury at the furthest point of the site, will quickly reveal missing phone numbers, a locked gate, a vehicle without fuel, or a team that does not know who is in charge. Record what was learned and fix it. Step 8: Review after every incident After any real emergency or near miss, meet within a few days to ask what worked, what took too long and what to change. Update the plan, the contact sheet and the training. Keep a log of incidents and response times so management can see trends. A one-page checklist Risks and distances documented for each location Three named facilities, with hours and capabilities confirmed First aiders, kits and roles in place Emergency contacts in every vehicle and work area Transport arrangement tested, with authority to call it out Hospital informed and expectations written into the agreement Annual drill completed and recorded Incident review process in place This article is general guidance, not a substitute for a professional health-and-safety assessment or for applicable regulations and client requirements. About this article. Prepared by the i.zino concept team in Warri, Delta State, Nigeria, from our work on medical and business retainership introductions, registration support and customer-service projects. Published 8 November 2026. Spotted an error or an out-of-date detail? Email i.zinoconcept@gmail.com and we will correct it. See our editorial note . Need tailored advice? If you have a specific retainership, registration or customer-service challenge, we can discuss it directly. Contact Us More guides What a Medical Retainership Agreement Should Cover Medical Retainership or HMO? Choosing a Hospital for a Corporate Retainership Petroleum-Sector Registration: A Document Checklist A Simple Complaint-Handling Process for Small Businesses Pre-Employment and Periodic Medical Examinations"}, {"title": "What a Medical Retainership Agreement Should Cover: A Clause-by-Clause Guide", "url": "medical-retainership-agreement-guide.html", "desc": "A practical clause-by-clause guide to medical retainership agreements between Nigerian companies and private hospitals: scope, pricing models, response times, billing, confidentiality and termination.", "text": "Resources › Guide · Medical Retainership What a Medical Retainership Agreement Should Cover: A Clause-by-Clause Guide By the i.zino concept team · Published 8 November 2026 · 7 min read A medical retainership agreement is a contract under which a company, often in oil and gas or another large employer, designates a hospital or clinic to look after its staff's healthcare needs on agreed terms. For the company it brings predictable access and cost. For the hospital it brings a steady flow of patients and a clearer view of income. Both benefits depend on how well the agreement is written. Disputes in this area rarely come from bad faith. They usually come from vague wording: nobody wrote down who is covered, how fast the hospital must respond, or when an invoice is due. The clauses below are the ones worth settling in writing before the first patient walks in. 1. Parties and who is covered Name the company and the hospital precisely, using the registered names. Then define the covered population: permanent staff only, or also contract staff, dependants and next of kin? If dependants are included, say how many per employee and whether spouses and children are treated the same way. Agree how the company will notify the hospital of new joiners and leavers, because an out-of-date staff list is the most common source of billing disagreements. 2. Scope of services List what is included and what is not. Typical headings are outpatient consultations, laboratory and imaging, pharmacy, inpatient care, surgery, maternity, dental and eye care, and emergencies. Be equally clear about exclusions, such as cosmetic procedures or treatment abroad, and about services the hospital cannot provide itself and will refer onward. Where the company's work involves pre-employment or periodic medical examinations, or fitness checks before field or offshore assignments, put those in the scope explicitly, with the format of the resulting report. 3. Pricing model There are three common ways to price a retainership, and many agreements combine them: Flat retainer. The company pays a fixed monthly or annual sum for a defined bundle of services. It is easy to budget but needs a clear limit on what the bundle covers. Fee-for-service at an agreed tariff. The company pays for each service used, at a price list attached to the contract. It is flexible, but costs vary with usage. Per-head (capitation). The company pays a set amount per covered person. It transfers some utilisation risk to the hospital, so the hospital will want realistic assumptions and sometimes a cap on high-cost cases. Whichever model is chosen, attach the tariff as a schedule and state how and when it can be reviewed. Silent price increases are a quick way to damage a relationship. 4. Service levels and response times This is the clause that turns a hospital listing into a working partnership. Set measurable expectations: emergency access around the clock, maximum waiting time for scheduled appointments, turnaround times for laboratory results, and how corporate patients are prioritised or fast-tracked. If the company's staff work at remote sites, agree how ambulance or transfer support is arranged and who pays for it. 5. Referrals and authorisations No private hospital can do everything. Agree how referrals to specialists or tertiary facilities are handled, who authorises high-cost procedures, and what the hospital must do in an emergency when pre-approval is not possible. A short, named list of contacts on each side (usually an operations or HR lead for the company and a corporate-services officer for the hospital) saves many hours. 6. Billing and payment State the invoicing cycle, what must accompany each invoice (for example, patient identification and a service breakdown), the payment period, and how disputed items are handled so that a disagreement over one line does not freeze payment of the rest. Late payment is a real strain on a private hospital's cash flow, so a clear payment period, and a sensible remedy if it is missed, protects both sides. 7. Confidentiality and records Health information is sensitive personal data. The agreement should say who may see what: the company should normally receive fitness-for-work conclusions and utilisation summaries, not clinical detail about individual employees, unless the employee has consented. Both sides should also understand their obligations under Nigeria's data protection law and handle records accordingly. 8. Reporting and review A short monthly or quarterly utilisation report (visits, common conditions in aggregate, turnaround performance, complaints) lets the company check value for money and lets the hospital plan capacity. Build in a scheduled review meeting, at least annually, where pricing and service levels can be adjusted by agreement. 9. Professional standards, liability and insurance The hospital should confirm that it and its practitioners hold the registrations and licences required to practise, and that it carries appropriate professional indemnity cover. Clinical decisions remain the hospital's responsibility; the company's obligations are payment and information. Say so plainly. 10. Term, renewal and termination Set the start date, the length of the term and how renewal works. Include a notice period for termination by either side, and a transition clause so that patients in the middle of treatment are not left without care if the relationship ends. Finally, name the governing law and a practical way to resolve disputes, such as escalation to senior management followed by mediation or arbitration, before anyone considers court. What the hospital gains, and why it should push for detail For a private hospital, a corporate retainership offers predictable patient volume and a clearer cash-flow picture, and it lets the facility standardise how a particular client's staff are seen. That only works if the hospital can staff and equip itself for the load it has agreed to. Hospitals should therefore resist open-ended promises on response times or caseload and ask for realistic projections of staff numbers and expected usage before signing. A note on drafting The structure above is a checklist, not a template. Every arrangement has its own commercial reality, and the final contract should be reviewed by a lawyer qualified in Nigeria. Where we facilitate introductions between companies and hospitals, our role is to help both sides arrive at the table with these questions already answered, so that the formal drafting is quicker and the working relationship starts on firm ground. About this article. Prepared by the i.zino concept team in Warri, Delta State, Nigeria, from our work on medical and business retainership introductions, registration support and customer-service projects. Published 8 November 2026. Spotted an error or an out-of-date detail? Email i.zinoconcept@gmail.com and we will correct it. See our editorial note . Need tailored advice? If you have a specific retainership, registration or customer-service challenge, we can discuss it directly. Contact Us More guides Medical Retainership or HMO? Choosing a Hospital for a Corporate Retainership Petroleum-Sector Registration: A Document Checklist A Simple Complaint-Handling Process for Small Businesses Pre-Employment and Periodic Medical Examinations A Medical Emergency Plan for Remote and Field Staff"}, {"title": "Medical Retainership or HMO? How Nigerian Companies Can Decide", "url": "medical-retainership-vs-hmo-nigeria.html", "desc": "Should a Nigerian company contract hospitals directly through a retainership or buy cover through an HMO? A neutral comparison of cost control, flexibility, administration and risk, with a simple decision framework.", "text": "Resources › Guide · Employee Healthcare Medical Retainership or HMO? How Nigerian Companies Can Decide By the i.zino concept team · Published 8 November 2026 · 6 min read When a company decides to look after its employees' healthcare, two routes come up again and again. The first is to buy cover through a Health Maintenance Organisation (HMO), which contracts a network of hospitals and manages claims. The second is to contract one or more hospitals directly under a medical retainership. Neither is better in every situation. The right answer depends on where your people work, how many there are and how much control you want. How each model works HMO cover. The employer pays the HMO a premium, usually per person. Staff then use hospitals in the HMO's network, and the HMO handles authorisations and pays the hospitals. The HMO carries part of the financial risk of high usage, in return for the premium. Medical retainership. The employer agrees terms directly with a chosen hospital or small group of hospitals: services, prices, response times and payment. The employer carries the usage risk (unless the contract is a capped or capitated arrangement) but also gets more say in how the service is delivered. Our guide to what a retainership agreement should cover sets out the clauses involved. Where each tends to work well Factor HMO Direct retainership Workforce spread Suits staff spread across many towns or states, because networks are broad Suits staff concentrated near one or two facilities Cost predictability High: a fixed premium, though it is reviewed at renewal Depends on the pricing model; a flat or capped retainer is predictable, fee-for-service is not Control of service levels Limited: the HMO sets network terms Greater: response times and priority access can be written in Administrative load Low for the employer, as the HMO handles claims Higher: the employer or its adviser manages invoices and utilisation Staff choice Wide choice within the network Narrower: usually the named hospitals Occupational health needs Often limited to what the plan offers Easier to tailor, such as pre-deployment medicals and site support Relationship Employer deals with an insurer-type intermediary Employer deals directly with the care provider Questions to ask before choosing Where do our people actually work and live? A team based around one city can be served well by a local hospital arrangement. A workforce spread across the country needs reach. How much do we need to control response times? Companies with shift work, remote sites or safety-critical roles often want guaranteed emergency access and fast-tracked medicals, which are easier to negotiate directly. How stable is our headcount? Premium-based cover scales per head. A direct arrangement works best when staff numbers and usage are reasonably predictable. Do we have the capacity to manage it? Someone has to review invoices, track utilisation and hold review meetings. If you have no one for this, either choose the HMO route or use an adviser. What have staff complained about before? Long waiting times, refused authorisations and limited choice point to different answers. The hybrid option These are not mutually exclusive. Some employers keep an HMO plan for general cover across the country and add a retainership with a hospital near their main base for emergencies, occupational health examinations and priority access. If you go this way, define clearly which arrangement pays for what, so that bills do not fall between the two. What to check on either route That the provider, whether HMO or hospital, is properly licensed and in good standing with the relevant regulator. How complaints and disputes are escalated, and how fast. What happens to a patient in the middle of treatment if the arrangement ends. How staff data is protected and who can see it. Our view We do not push one model. Our work is introducing companies, particularly in oil and gas, to hospitals for retainership arrangements, so we naturally see the cases where a direct relationship adds value: concentrated workforces, strong occupational-health needs and a wish for named contacts. But where a workforce is dispersed, or a company wants minimal administration, an HMO may well be the better fit. If you are weighing the two, talk to us and we will walk through your situation honestly, including when a retainership is not the right answer. This article is general information, not legal, financial or insurance advice. Confirm current regulatory requirements and provider terms before deciding. About this article. Prepared by the i.zino concept team in Warri, Delta State, Nigeria, from our work on medical and business retainership introductions, registration support and customer-service projects. Published 8 November 2026. Spotted an error or an out-of-date detail? Email i.zinoconcept@gmail.com and we will correct it. See our editorial note . Need tailored advice? If you have a specific retainership, registration or customer-service challenge, we can discuss it directly. Contact Us More guides What a Medical Retainership Agreement Should Cover Choosing a Hospital for a Corporate Retainership Petroleum-Sector Registration: A Document Checklist A Simple Complaint-Handling Process for Small Businesses Pre-Employment and Periodic Medical Examinations A Medical Emergency Plan for Remote and Field Staff"}, {"title": "Petroleum-Sector Registration in Nigeria: A Practical Document Checklist for Service Companies", "url": "petroleum-sector-registration-checklist-nigeria.html", "desc": "A practical, up-to-date checklist for Nigerian service companies preparing to register for oil and gas work: CAC, tax, NSITF, industry permits from NUPRC or NMDPRA, Nigerian content registration and a compliance file.", "text": "Resources › Business Setup · Nigeria Petroleum-Sector Registration in Nigeria: A Practical Document Checklist for Service Companies By the i.zino concept team · Published 8 November 2026 · 8 min read Companies that want to supply goods or services to the oil and gas industry in Nigeria quickly find that registration is not one task but a chain of them, where each document depends on another. Getting the order right saves weeks. Below is the sequence we use as a working framework, based on supporting clients through industry registration, including NSITF employer registration and clearance documentation. Check for changes. Regulators, forms, fees and portals change. Treat this as a map of the process, and confirm current requirements directly with each agency or with a qualified adviser before you submit. First, a correction many older guides still get wrong If you are reading a guide that tells you to apply to the Department of Petroleum Resources (DPR), it is out of date. The government scrapped the DPR in 2021 and created two new regulators: the Nigerian Upstream Petroleum Regulatory Commission (NUPRC) for upstream operations, and the Nigerian Midstream and Downstream Petroleum Regulatory Authority (NMDPRA) for midstream and downstream. The right regulator for your permit depends on the kind of work you will do, so confirm which one applies to your service category. Old certificates may still carry DPR branding, and clients sometimes ask for them, but new applications go to the successor bodies. Similarly, the Federal Inland Revenue Service (FIRS) became the Nigeria Revenue Service (NRS) on 1 January 2026 under the new tax laws. Names on forms and portals are changing, and some guides still use the old ones. Stage 1: Your corporate foundation (CAC) Everything else rests on a properly incorporated company. Have these ready and consistent: Certificate of incorporation from the Corporate Affairs Commission (CAC) Memorandum and Articles of Association Particulars of directors and shareholders, including any information the CAC requires on persons with significant control A current CAC status report (many pre-qualification exercises ask for a recent one) Evidence of your registered office address Make sure the company name, address and director names are written identically on every document. Small differences are a common reason for rejected submissions. Stage 2: Tax registration and compliance Tax Identification Number (TIN) Evidence of tax compliance, such as a tax clearance certificate or its current equivalent, issued by the relevant federal or state revenue authority Filed returns and, where required, audited financial statements With the changes to the national tax framework, confirm the current name of the issuing authority and the current form of the compliance evidence before you apply. Stage 3: Employer registrations NSITF (Employees' Compensation Scheme). Under the Employees' Compensation Act 2010, employers contribute 1% of total monthly payroll to the Employees' Compensation Fund, and the contribution comes from the employer, not from staff deductions. Registration uses NSITF's employer forms, and the compliance certificate is issued on submission of the request letter, your CAC certificate, the completed forms and evidence of payment. NSITF indicates a typical processing time of about five working days once everything is complete and verified. Because the certificate depends on your CAC documents and on payroll records, start this early. Other employer obligations. Depending on your size and structure, tenders often also ask for evidence of pension compliance and of Industrial Training Fund (ITF) contributions. Check whether and how they apply to you. Stage 4: The industry permit from the regulator Once the foundation is in place, you apply to the relevant regulator for the permit that matches your service category. Categories differ in the technical capacity expected. Typical supporting material includes: A company profile and organisation chart CVs and certifications of key technical staff A list of equipment, tools or facilities relevant to the service Your health, safety and environment (HSE) policy Evidence of premises A bank reference and recent financial statements Your NSITF, tax and CAC documents from the earlier stages Requirements vary by category, so ask the regulator for the current checklist for the specific service you plan to offer. Stage 5: Nigerian content registration Operators and major contractors usually expect service companies to be registered with the Nigerian Content Development and Monitoring Board (NCDMB), commonly through the Nigerian Oil and Gas Industry Content Joint Qualification System. Registration there is often a pre-qualification requirement for tenders, and it also asks for evidence of ownership, local content and capacity. Confirm the current process with the Board. Stage 6: Build a compliance file and a renewal calendar Many certificates and permits have to be renewed periodically. Keep one organised file, paper and digital, with every certificate, its issue date and its expiry, and set reminders well ahead. An expired certificate discovered during a tender is far more expensive than a diary entry. The mistakes that cause repeat submissions Following an old checklist. Using DPR-era instructions or former agency names. Inconsistent details. Names, addresses or directors that differ across documents. Wrong order. Applying for an item before its prerequisite exists, for example NSITF clearance before the CAC documents are complete. Incomplete payroll or financial records. These are needed for NSITF and for pre-qualification. Missing expiry dates. Submitting a certificate that lapses before the process finishes. When to get help If you are registering for the first time, or you have been rejected and are not sure why, an adviser who has done the process before can save months. Documentation and registration support is part of what we do at i.zino concept, so you are welcome to contact us with details of your company and the service you want to offer. General information only, last reviewed 8 November 2026. It is not legal, tax or regulatory advice, and requirements change. Verify with the relevant agency before acting. About this article. Prepared by the i.zino concept team in Warri, Delta State, Nigeria, from our work on medical and business retainership introductions, registration support and customer-service projects. Published 8 November 2026. Spotted an error or an out-of-date detail? Email i.zinoconcept@gmail.com and we will correct it. See our editorial note . Need tailored advice? If you have a specific retainership, registration or customer-service challenge, we can discuss it directly. Contact Us More guides What a Medical Retainership Agreement Should Cover Medical Retainership or HMO? Choosing a Hospital for a Corporate Retainership A Simple Complaint-Handling Process for Small Businesses Pre-Employment and Periodic Medical Examinations A Medical Emergency Plan for Remote and Field Staff"}, {"title": "Pre-Employment and Periodic Medical Examinations: A Practical Guide for Employers", "url": "pre-employment-and-periodic-medical-examinations-guide.html", "desc": "What employers in Nigeria should specify for pre-employment and periodic medical examinations: purpose, typical components, fitness outcomes, confidentiality, frequency and how to put it in a hospital retainership.", "text": "Resources › Guide · Occupational Health Pre-Employment and Periodic Medical Examinations: A Practical Guide for Employers By the i.zino concept team · Published 8 November 2026 · 6 min read Many employers, especially in oil and gas, construction and other physically demanding sectors, require staff to pass a medical examination before starting work and at intervals afterwards. Done well, these examinations protect workers, help the employer place people in suitable roles, and give an early warning of health problems. Done badly, they become a paperwork exercise, or worse, a source of disputes about privacy and fairness. This guide sets out what to decide before you commission them. Start with the purpose An examination should answer a specific question: is this person fit to do this job safely? That is different from a full diagnostic check-up. Being clear about the purpose keeps the scope reasonable, avoids collecting information you do not need, and makes the outcome easier to act on. Pre-employment (or pre-placement) examinations establish a baseline and confirm fitness for the role before the person starts. Periodic examinations monitor health at agreed intervals, especially where the work involves particular exposures or physical demands. Return-to-work examinations follow a long illness or injury. Special assessments apply before assignments with unusual demands, such as remote or offshore work, where access to care is limited. Match the content to the job's risks There is no single standard package. A sensible examination is built from the job's real hazards, and the examining doctor has the final say on what is clinically appropriate. Common elements include: Medical history and a general physical examination Vision and hearing tests, particularly for drivers, machine operators and noisy environments Blood pressure, weight and basic laboratory tests Chest imaging or lung-function tests where dust, fumes or chemicals are involved Specific checks linked to the role, such as the physical capacity for heavy lifting or working at height Avoid adding tests simply because they are available. Every extra test adds cost and collects more personal information. Define the possible outcomes The report the employer receives should be a clear fitness conclusion, not a medical file. A typical set of categories is: Outcome Meaning Fit No health reason to prevent the person doing the job. Fit with restrictions Can do the job with stated adjustments, for example no work at height or limited lifting. Temporarily unfit Should not start or continue until a condition is treated or reviewed. Unfit The condition makes the job unsafe for the person or others, even with adjustments. Agree in advance what the company will do for each outcome, including how an employee can ask for a second opinion. Decisions based on a medical result should be fair, consistent and documented, and employers should take legal advice before acting on an adverse result. Protect confidentiality Medical information is among the most sensitive personal data. The employer usually needs the fitness conclusion and any workplace restrictions, not the diagnosis. The doctor should explain this to the employee before the examination, and the employee should know who will see the report. Records should be stored securely by the hospital, separately from the personnel file, and handled in line with Nigeria's data protection law. Set a sensible frequency How often to repeat an examination depends on the risk. Roles with significant exposures or physical demands may need annual checks, while lower-risk office roles may need far less. Age, existing conditions and regulatory or client requirements can all affect the schedule. Ask the examining doctor to advise, then write the schedule into your policy so everyone is treated alike. Plan the logistics Booking and turnaround. Agree how appointments are made, how long reports take, and how urgent cases are handled. Volume. Large intakes or shutdown periods put pressure on a hospital, so give notice. Cost. Decide who pays and whether examinations are priced per package or per test. Standard forms. A standard report template makes results comparable and easy to file. Put it in the retainership If you have a medical retainership with a hospital, the examination programme should be a named schedule in it: the packages, prices, turnaround times, report format and confidentiality rules. Our guide to what a retainership agreement should cover shows where it fits, and our checklist for choosing a hospital lists what to look for in diagnostics and staffing before you commit. Common mistakes Requiring the same extensive package for every role, regardless of risk Letting the employer see detailed diagnoses Having no process for staff who receive an adverse result Failing to keep records that show the programme was applied consistently General information only. It is not medical or legal advice. Examination content and employment decisions should be guided by qualified doctors and your legal adviser. About this article. Prepared by the i.zino concept team in Warri, Delta State, Nigeria, from our work on medical and business retainership introductions, registration support and customer-service projects. Published 8 November 2026. Spotted an error or an out-of-date detail? Email i.zinoconcept@gmail.com and we will correct it. See our editorial note . Need tailored advice? If you have a specific retainership, registration or customer-service challenge, we can discuss it directly. Contact Us More guides What a Medical Retainership Agreement Should Cover Medical Retainership or HMO? Choosing a Hospital for a Corporate Retainership Petroleum-Sector Registration: A Document Checklist A Simple Complaint-Handling Process for Small Businesses A Medical Emergency Plan for Remote and Field Staff"}, {"title": "Privacy Policy", "url": "privacy.html", "desc": "How i.zino concept collects, uses and protects personal data, including cookies, Google Analytics and Google AdSense advertising, and your rights under Nigerian data protection law.", "text": "Privacy Policy Last updated 8 November 2026 This policy explains what personal information i.zino concept (\"we\", \"us\") collects through izinoconcept.github.io , how we use it, who we share it with and what rights you have. We are based in Warri, Delta State, Nigeria, and you can contact us at i.zinoconcept@gmail.com . 1. Information we collect Information you give us. If you use our contact form or email us, we receive your name, email address, organisation (optional), the subject and the content of your message. Information collected automatically. When you visit, we and our service providers may collect technical data such as your IP address, browser and device type, pages viewed, time spent and approximate location. This is collected through cookies and similar technologies (see our Cookie Policy ) and through server logs kept by our hosting provider. 2. How we use information To respond to your enquiries and provide the services you request. To understand how the site is used and to improve its content and performance. To show advertising on the site and measure how it performs, as explained in section 4. To keep the site secure and to meet legal obligations. We do not sell your personal information. 3. Services we use Hosting: GitHub Pages hosts the site and may keep standard server logs. Contact form: FormSubmit receives form submissions and forwards them to our email inbox. Analytics: Google Analytics and Google Tag Manager, to measure traffic and usage. Advertising: Google AdSense, described below. Fonts: Google Fonts, which loads font files from Google's servers and therefore receives your IP address. These providers process data under their own privacy policies. 4. Advertising and Google AdSense We use Google AdSense to display advertisements on this site. Third-party vendors, including Google, use cookies to serve ads based on a user's prior visits to this website or other websites. Google's use of advertising cookies enables it and its partners to serve ads to you based on your visit to this site and/or other sites on the internet. You can opt out of personalised advertising by visiting Google Ads Settings . You can also opt out of some third-party vendors' use of cookies for personalised advertising at aboutads.info . For more information on how Google uses data from sites that use its services, see How Google uses information from sites or apps that use our services . Where the law requires it, for example for visitors in the European Economic Area, the United Kingdom and Switzerland, we ask for your consent before cookies for personalised advertising are used. 5. Legal basis Under the Nigeria Data Protection Act 2023, we process personal data where you have given consent (for example when you submit the contact form or accept cookies), where it is necessary to respond to your request, or where we have a legitimate interest in running and improving our website and where that interest is not overridden by your rights. 6. Your rights Subject to applicable law, you may ask us to confirm whether we hold personal data about you, to give you access to it, to correct it, to delete it, to restrict or object to certain processing, and to withdraw consent at any time. To exercise these rights, email i.zinoconcept@gmail.com . If you are unhappy with how we handle your data, you may also complain to the Nigeria Data Protection Commission. 7. How long we keep information We keep enquiry messages for as long as needed to deal with your request and for reasonable business and record-keeping purposes, after which we delete them. Analytics and advertising data are kept according to the settings and policies of the relevant providers. 8. International transfers and security Some of our service providers, including Google, GitHub and FormSubmit, process data on servers outside Nigeria. We choose established providers and rely on their security measures, but no internet transmission is completely secure. 9. Children This site is aimed at businesses and professionals and is not directed at children. We do not knowingly collect personal data from children. 10. Links to other sites The site links to external websites such as Facebook and YouTube. We are not responsible for their content or privacy practices. 11. Changes to this policy We may update this policy from time to time. The \"Last updated\" date above shows the latest version. 12. Contact Questions about this policy: i.zinoconcept@gmail.com , or use our contact page ."}, {"title": "Projects & Portfolio", "url": "projects.html", "desc": "Selected projects and client work by i.zino concept – retainership agreements, registrations and service introductions.", "text": "Projects & Portfolio Examples of retainership work, registrations and client introductions we have supported. Medical Retainership Agreements Facilitated medical retainership and service-line agreements between oil & gas related companies and private medical centres. Examples include agreements involving Star Offshore Energy, BJ Bison Ventures and partner hospitals such as Anis Medical Centre and Hommies Medical Centre. Focus: Clear service expectations, documentation and ongoing operational alignment. Industry Registration Support Assisted SMASA Nigeria Enterprises with successful registration at the Department of Petroleum Resources (DPR, whose functions have since passed to the NUPRC and NMDPRA) to provide services to the oil industry, together with related NSITF employer registration and clearance documentation. Focus: Regulatory readiness and formal market access. Healthcare Partners We Have Worked With Over time we have introduced or supported relationships involving a wide range of private hospitals and clinics. Below is a representative selection: Ebony Clinic and Maternity Twins Clinic & Hospital Coastal Specialist Clinic Limited Unique Health Medical Centre God Reigns Clinic & Maternity Day Spring Clinic St. Georges Specialist Clinic Xrist Ville Hill Hospital Lime Light Clinic and Hospital LTD Shepherd Hospital & Fertility Centre Kings Clinics/Hospital Visionext Eye Care Clinic Aviel Specialist Clinic & Fertility Centre Harvard Specialist Hospital Radah Bay Hospital New Age Clinic Eternal Salvation Medical Hospital/Maternity LTD Curatio Medicare Specialist Hospital Long Life Hospital & Maternity Services Fair View Hospital Limited Delta Procare Clinic/Hospital First Rank Hospital Limited Hope Radiology Centre Lakeland Specialist Clinic Mordicare Hospital Limited Divine Moving-Star Clinic & Maternity St. Melvin's Hospital Vertimon Medical Centre Ltd Bryant’s Clinic & Maternity Limited Unity Clinic Pearl Clinic 8EightMedical Zeenella Medical Centre This list is illustrative of the breadth of healthcare providers with whom we have engaged. Specific commercial details remain confidential between the parties. Interested in a similar introduction or agreement? Discuss Your Project"}, {"title": "Client Ratings & Reviews", "url": "reviews.html", "desc": "Genuine client reviews of i.zino concept, with average rating and approval percentage calculated from published reviews only, and a form to share your own experience.", "text": "Client Ratings & Reviews Honest feedback from real clients. Client Ratings & Reviews What real clients say about working with us. Ratings and the approval percentage are calculated automatically from genuine, published reviews only. We do not edit ratings, pay for reviews or publish anonymous testimonials. Worked with us? Leave a review Your name * Organisation / role Your rating * Select… 5 – Excellent 4 – Good 3 – Average 2 – Poor 1 – Very poor Your review * I agree that my name, organisation and review may be published on this website. * Submit review Reviews are checked before publication. We publish honest feedback whether positive or critical. See our Privacy Policy ."}, {"title": "Services", "url": "services.html", "desc": "Services offered by i.zino concept: medical retainership introductions, customer service operations, consultancy, business registration and general contracts.", "text": "Our Services Practical solutions for retainership, customer service and business support. Medical & Business Retainership Introductions We connect oil & gas operators, corporate employers and other organisations with suitable hospitals and clinics. Our role includes facilitating discussions with heads of operations and supporting the preparation of medical retainership agreements that meet both clinical and commercial requirements. Typical outcomes include signed retainership contracts, clearer service-level expectations and long-term partnerships between energy-sector clients and healthcare providers. Customer Service Operations We help organisations design and run customer-service processes that satisfy clients and encourage repeat business. This covers enquiry handling, complaint resolution, communication standards and the day-to-day systems that protect reputation and loyalty. Whether you need a complete service framework or targeted improvements, we focus on practical changes that staff can apply immediately. Consultancy Services We provide advisory support across different stages of business operations—from structuring new service offerings to improving existing client-facing processes. Our consultancy is grounded in local market knowledge and a results-oriented approach. Business Registration & Documentation Support with company registration, regulatory documentation and related compliance steps that allow businesses to operate formally and bid for contracts. We have assisted clients with registrations relevant to the petroleum industry and other sectors. General Contracts & Workforce Support Assistance with contract preparation, service agreements and workforce-related delivery where structured commercial arrangements are required. We aim for clear, workable documents that protect all parties. Not sure which service fits your situation? Talk to Us Related guides What a medical retainership agreement should cover How to choose a hospital for a corporate retainership Petroleum-sector registration: a document checklist A simple complaint-handling process for small businesses"}, {"title": "Terms & Conditions", "url": "terms.html", "desc": "Terms and Conditions of i.zino concept website.", "text": "Terms & Conditions Last updated: September 2026 By accessing and using the website of i.zino concept you agree to these terms. If you do not agree, please do not use the site. 1. About the Website This website provides information about i.zino concept and our services. Content is for general information only and does not constitute a binding offer or professional advice unless we expressly agree otherwise in writing. 2. Use of the Site You may use the site for lawful purposes only. You must not attempt to disrupt the site, introduce malware, or use it in any way that could harm us or other users. 3. Intellectual Property Text, design, logos and other materials on this site are owned by or licensed to i.zino concept. You may view and print pages for personal or internal business use. You may not copy, modify or redistribute content for commercial purposes without our prior written consent. 4. Enquiries and Services Submitting an enquiry through the contact form does not create a contract. Any services we provide will be subject to separate terms agreed between us and the client. 5. Disclaimer We take care to keep information accurate, but we do not warrant that the site is complete, up to date or free from errors. We are not liable for any loss arising from reliance on information published on the site, to the fullest extent permitted by law. 6. Third-Party Links The site may contain links to external websites. We are not responsible for the content or practices of those sites. 7. Changes We may update these terms at any time. Continued use of the site after changes constitutes acceptance of the revised terms. 8. Governing Law These terms are governed by the laws of Nigeria. Any disputes will be subject to the jurisdiction of the Nigerian courts. 9. Contact Questions about these terms can be sent via our Contact page."}];
